# State & Data

Server state and client state are different. Treat them differently.

## State management

- **Server state → TanStack Query.** Anything fetched from a backend.
- **Client state → Zustand.** UI state, flow state machines, ephemeral UI flags.
- Keep state close to where it is used. Lift only when truly shared.
- Avoid giant global stores. Slice by feature.
- Never duplicate derived state — compute it from source.
- Never mutate state directly. Prefer immutable patterns (`set(s => …)`).

```ts
// ❌ BAD — derived state stored separately, can drift
const [phone, setPhone] = useState('');
const [isValid, setIsValid] = useState(false);
useEffect(() => setIsValid(phone.length === 10), [phone]);

// ✅ GOOD — derive on render
const [phone, setPhone] = useState('');
const isValid = phone.length === 10;
```

## API layer

- Centralize API configuration in `src/api/` (base URL, headers, timeout, abort).
- Never call `fetch` directly inside a component. Wrap every endpoint in a typed
  function under `features/<name>/api/`.
- Type both request and response (`SendOtpRequest`, `SendOtpResponse`).
- Validate API responses with Zod at the edge (`schema.parse(json)`) before
  handing data to the rest of the app.
- Map every backend error code → UI error via a central parser
  (`parseAuthServiceError`). Never display raw server messages in production.
- Errors flow through a consistent typed error class (`ApiError`), never raw thrown strings.

## Async patterns

- Use `async/await`, never raw `.then` chains.
- Always handle loading, success, AND error explicitly.
- Never ignore rejected promises. `void promise.catch(...)` if you really must.
- Retry only what's safe to retry (idempotent reads, network-only failures).
  Never auto-retry side-effecting writes (`/otp/send`).
- Debounce expensive user actions (search, autosave). Throttle high-frequency
  events (scroll, gesture).
